  <h2>1. Frame the question</h2>
  <p>
    A focused question decides everything after it. In health research, PICO (population, intervention, comparison,
    outcome) is the usual frame; in software engineering and the social sciences, a clear scope with inclusion and
    exclusion criteria does the same job.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Write a protocol</h2>
  <p>
    Record the question, databases, search strings, criteria and planned analysis before you start. Health reviews
    register it on PROSPERO; others can publish it on OSF. A protocol protects you from changing the rules halfway.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Search, and document the search</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Search several sources: subject databases, plus broad ones such as Crossref, Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex.</li>
    <li>Save every query string, date and result count: reviewers will ask.</li>
    <li>Remove duplicates by <a href={href('glossary/doi/')}>DOI</a> first, then by title.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Screen in two stages</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Title and abstract:</strong> quick include, exclude or maybe against your criteria.</li>
    <li><strong>Full text:</strong> read the paper and record the reason for every exclusion.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>5. Snowball</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={href('glossary/snowballing/')}>Snowballing</a>: for every included paper, check its references (backward) and the papers citing it (forward). Screen what you find
    with the same criteria, and count it separately in your flow diagram.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Extract, appraise, synthesise</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Extract into a fixed form, and keep the page each number came from.</li>
    <li>Appraise quality with a tool suited to the study design.</li>
    <li>Synthesise narratively, or with a meta-analysis when studies are comparable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Report with PRISMA</h2>
  <p>
    Follow the <a href={href('glossary/prisma/')}>PRISMA</a> 2020 checklist and include the flow diagram. The numbers are easy if you counted as you went,
    and painful to rebuild at the end. See also <a href={href('systematic-reviews/')}>PaperLab for systematic reviews</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
